<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ============================================================================
         CDE v1 BASELINE, part 3 of 3: the public_holiday bootstrap-order guard.

         THIS IS THE VERSION 1 BASELINE. Everything said in parts 1 and 2 about the
         absent re-run guards holds here too, with ONE exception that this whole file
         is about, and that exception is why the changeset below still carries a
         precondition while nothing else in this baseline does.

         public_holiday is NOT CDE's table. dcre-hcs owns it and is its only writer
         (R-04), and it is read by live services in BOTH families, so its home does
         not move. What changes here is only HOW it is declared, never whether: the
         raw-SQL body is now a typed createTable with a rollback, so the DDL is
         visible to static analysis and portable across dialects.

         WHY THE GUARD STAYS. dcre_col is shared, and HCS declares the same table in
         its own changelog (hcs 001-hcs.xml, changeset 001-public-holiday-hcs). CDE
         is launched per arrival while HCS runs on a 6h clock, so on a freshly
         recreated database either one can reach Liquibase first. That makes this an
         object that CAN exist on an empty schema, which is exactly the condition the
         other guards in this baseline failed to meet and this one meets. An
         unguarded createTable here would crashloop CDE on every ordering where HCS
         wins the race.

         MARK_RAN rather than CONTINUE is correct for THIS dependency, and the
         distinction is the A-79 lesson. CONTINUE is for a dependency that has not
         bootstrapped YET and will exist later, where recording the skip would make
         it permanent. Here the guarded object is the thing this changeset creates:
         if it already exists the work is genuinely done and will never need doing
         again, so recording it as applied is the truth.

         COLUMN SET IS THE OWNER'S, VERBATIM, and holiday_date stays holiday_date.
         It repeats its own table name, which the column-naming rule forbids, and it
         is NOT autocorrected here on purpose: HCS is the owner and the writer, this
         file is a downstream guard, and a rename applied on one side of a race would
         hand the database two incompatible shapes depending on which pod started
         first. Correcting it is an HCS-owned fleet change, not a CDE one.

         The UNIQUE constraint gains a name, which a typed declaration requires;
         HCS's raw-SQL form leaves the equivalent constraint auto-named, so the name
         differs by which service created the table. Nothing depends on the name:
         the ON CONFLICT target used by CDE's fail-closed holiday check is the column
         list.
         ============================================================================ -->

    <changeSet id="cde-v1-public-holiday-guard" author="dcre">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="public_holiday"/></not>
        </preConditions>

        <createTable tableName="public_holiday">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="country" type="VARCHAR(2)">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="holiday_date" type="DATE">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="local_name" type="VARCHAR(128)"/>
            <column name="name" type="VARCHAR(128)"/>
            <column name="is_global" type="BOOLEAN"/>
            <column name="version" type="BIGINT" defaultValueNumeric="0">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="public_holiday" columnNames="country, holiday_date"
                             constraintName="uq_public_holiday_country_date"/>
        <!--
          Deliberately EMPTY, and that is the honest option for a table this service
          does not own. A dropTable here would be correct only on the orderings where
          CDE won the race and created it. On the orderings where HCS won, this
          changeset is recorded MARK_RAN having created nothing, and rolling it back
          would drop the OWNER's table out from under HCS and every reader in both
          families. Liquibase gives no way to make the rollback conditional on which
          branch of the precondition was taken, so the safe half is the only half
          that can be declared.

          Reverting this table is an HCS operation against HCS's own changelog.
        -->
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
